+| 30 | `lkhznum.c` (new), `luaconf.h` | (web) `lua_strx2number` is `khz_strx2number`: a correctly rounded hexadecimal-numeral reader (round to nearest, ties to even), the same on every target (wasi-libc's `strtod` is off by one ulp for long hex mantissas; glibc 2.39's is wrong for 4 in 2 000 000 random subnormal hex inputs). `lua_number2strx` is `khz_number2strx`: `%a`/`%A` with glibc's output for subnormals ("0x0.<hex>p-1022", precision rounding ties to even, flags/width) and the C library's for everything else (glibc and musl agree there). |
+| 31 | `luaconf.h` | (web) `LUAL_BUFFERSIZE` is 1024 on every target (the LP64 value) instead of `16*sizeof(void*)*sizeof(lua_Number)`: buffer growth is charged to the budget (patch 20), so a 32-bit target grew and was charged at different sizes (`budget-edge` probe: `table.concat` of 700 bytes fit 2091 times natively, 815 times on wasm32). Unchanged on x86_64/aarch64. mlua-sys's Rust `LUAL_BUFFERSIZE` constant stays pointer-size based; neither mlua nor pin_script uses `luaL_Buffer` from Rust. |
+| 32 | `luaconf.h`, `lmathlib.c`, `llimits.h` | (web) With `KHZ_LUA_MATH` the transcendental functions (`sin cos tan asin acos atan2 exp log log2 log10 pow sinh cosh tanh`, so also `^`) call `khz_<name>`, provided by pin_script from the Rust `libm` crate (D9): the same bits natively and in the browser, independent of the C library and of CPU features (glibc picks FMA variants at run time). Exact functions (`floor ceil fabs fmod frexp ldexp sqrt`) keep the C library. |
 | — | `Cargo.toml` | Test target and dev-dependencies removed; only `lua-5.4.9/` kept. |
 
 Budget units are roughly "one VM instruction" (a few ns). All edits compile warning-free with `gcc -std=gnu99 -Wall -Wextra`.
